      "name": "menu-page-memoization",
      "type": "fix_bug",
      "title": "Fix the menu page memoization",
      "description": "Rice Rocket's menu page re-renders every dish card on each keystroke, a teammate's quick fix shows stale search results, and after a guest logs in the Add button still fills the guest's cart. The editor holds `app/restaurants/[slug]/menu-page.jsx` as plain functions (the grader provides React's `memo`, `useMemo` and `useCallback` with the same rules, and `render()` for writing a `<Component />`), with the four blocks of `MenuPage` missing, each marked by a `// [ ]` line; some pieces are last week's buggy versions. Fill every line so that: the expensive `filterMenu` runs only when the dishes or the query change and the results always match the query; a card renders again only when its own props change (narrowing the search or adding one dish to the cart re-renders nothing else); and Add always calls the `addToCart` of the current customer. Run renders the page through a sequence of updates and counts card renders and filter runs.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "outcomes": [
        "render-efficiency"
      ],
      "points": 30,
      "hints": [
        "For each prop a card receives, ask: is it the same object on the next render when nothing about this dish changed? A new function or a new array each render defeats `memo`.",
        "Look at the dependency arrays: every value the memoized function reads from the component's scope belongs in them, and nothing else.",
        "A product list with a stable remove handler looks like: `const visible = useMemo(() => sortProducts(products, sort), [products, sort]); const onRemove = useCallback((id) => removeItem(id), [removeItem]); return visible.map((p) => <Row key={p.id} product={p} selected={selectedIds.has(p.id)} onRemove={onRemove} />);`"
      ],
